    Spring Boot Starter supports Spring Boot 2.7, 3.5 and 4.0 (#2568)
    
    * Spring Boot Starter supports Spring Boot 4
    
    Keep the default build on Spring Boot 2.7 and select Spring Boot 4.0.6 with the
    spring-boot-4 Maven profile. Stop referencing Spring Boot 2 JDBC classes that
    moved in Spring Boot 4, bind the tracing data source with a starter-owned
    properties class, and run the starter tests against Spring Boot 4 in CI.
    
    Fixes #2506
    
    * Spring Boot Starter supports Spring Boot 2.7, 3.5 and 4.0
    
    Compile the starter against Spring Boot 2.7, the lowest supported line, so that the
    published artifacts run on all three lines. The spring-boot-3 and spring-boot-4
    profiles select the other lines; their versions are Maven properties in the root pom.
    
    CI builds and tests the starter and the Spring namespace module on Spring Boot 3 and
    4, and runs the artifacts compiled against Spring Boot 2.7 on those lines.
    
    Document the supported lines, their JDK requirements and the profiles.
    
    Fixes #2506
    
    * Document Spring Framework compatibility of Spring Namespace
    
    The namespace module is compiled against Spring Framework 5.3, and it is tested with
    Spring Framework 6.2 and 7.0, the Spring Framework versions of Spring Boot 3.5 and 4.0.
    
    * Support Spring Namespace jobs under GraalVM Native Image
    
    Spring AOT cannot process the private all-args constructor of JobConfiguration, nor the
    null, collection and bean definition arguments that the namespace parser passed to it.
    JobConfigurationFactoryBean now builds the job configuration from property values, and
    the tracing configuration is referenced by name instead of being copied as a bean definition.
    
    Add native tests for one-off and scheduled jobs defined by the Spring Namespace with RDB tracing.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Fix Spring Namespace native job test context
    
    The Spring Namespace defines its own tracing configuration, so the RDB tracing of the starter
    is disabled in SpringNamespaceJobTest. Otherwise the starter finds two TracingConfiguration beans
    while creating the jobs of application.yml. The test also scans the same packages as the other
    Spring Namespace native tests, so that those jobs can be created.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Assert the one-off job tracing by job name in Spring Namespace native test
    
    The assertion counted every JOB_EXECUTION_LOG row. The jobs of application.yml share the same
    tracing storage, so they could satisfy it without the one-off job writing any event.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Document GraalVM Native Image coverage of Spring Namespace
    
    The native tests cover the registry center tags memory, etcd and nacos, one-off and
    scheduled jobs defined by elasticjob:job with job-ref, and RDB tracing with
    elasticjob:rdb-tracing. The zookeeper tag, the job scanner, the snapshot, and the dataflow
    and script jobs are not covered by native tests yet.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Fix the error message of more than one TracingConfiguration beans
    
    The message named two classes that do not exist in this project, so its hint
    pointed nowhere. Build the class name from TracingConfiguration, and suggest the
    options that resolve the conflict: remove the extra bean, or do not set
    elasticjob.tracing.type to RDB.
    
    Add ElasticJobBootstrapConfigurationTest to assert the message when the
    application defines its own TracingConfiguration bean.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Compile the native test module with Java 17
    
    Spring AOT generates code that uses Java 9+ APIs, for example Map.of for the
    properties of a job definition in the Spring Namespace. The native test module
    compiles with native.java.version (17), while the rest of the project keeps
    java.version (8).
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Cover the dataflow and script jobs of the Spring Namespace in native tests
    
    The job elements of the Spring Namespace define a dataflow job with job-ref, and a
    script job with job-type SCRIPT and the script.command.line property. The native
    tests assert that the dataflow job processes data, and that the script job writes
    RDB tracing events.
    
    The script job runs echo from the PATH, so it does not depend on the working
    directory of the native test binary.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Cover the zookeeper, job-scanner and snapshot tags of the Spring Namespace in native tests
    
    The zookeeper test starts an in-process ZooKeeper server, which is shut down the same way as in
    SpringBootDTest. The job-scanner test checks a schedule job and a one-off job registered by the
    scanner. The snapshot test dumps job data through the dump port of the snapshot service.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Use a plain context in the Spring Namespace job-scanner native test
    
    The job-scanner test imported the auto-configuration through @SpringBootApplication. In the
    native image, refreshing the context failed while parsing WebMvcAutoConfiguration, because a
    class file it refers to is not available at runtime. Like AnnotationSpringBootTest, the context
    now imports only the namespace resource and runs without a web environment, because only the
    registration of the scanned jobs is asserted.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Keep the job scanner from registering annotation processors at native runtime
    
    After scanning, ClassPathBeanDefinitionScanner registers the annotation config processors into
    the registry. The Spring AOT context of a native image does not contain them, so the job
    scanner added a ConfigurationClassPostProcessor at runtime. That processor parsed the
    configuration classes again, and could not read the auto configuration or the
    @ImportResource of the test context in the native image.
    
    At native image runtime, JobScannerConfiguration no longer registers the annotation config
    processors. Other runtimes keep the existing behavior, in which the processors are
    registered like before.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Make the demo script of the native tests executable
    
    JavaTest, NacosJavaTest, MemoryJavaTest and EtcdJavaTest pass demo.sh to the script job by its
    path, and the job runs the file directly. The file was committed as 100644, so running it fails
    with Permission denied. These tests only schedule the job and shut it down, so the failure is
    not observed in CI.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Document the Spring Namespace coverage of GraalVM Native Image tests
    
    The native tests cover the registry center tags memory, etcd, nacos and zookeeper, the job
    scanner, the snapshot, RDB tracing, one-off, scheduled and non-streaming dataflow jobs defined
    by elasticjob:job with job-ref, and script jobs with job-type SCRIPT. Jobs with other job types,
    such as HTTP, are not covered yet.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Fix the Nacos registry center losing empty values and watch events
    
    Nacos rejects config content that is empty, and NacosIgnoredExceptionProvider ignores every
    NacosException. Writing an empty value, such as the sharding necessary flag or the data of a
    job node, therefore did nothing and reported no error, so the job never sharded and never ran.
    
    Store an empty value as a placeholder, and read the placeholder back as an empty value on every
    read path: direct reads, the cache, and the data and cache listeners.
    
    Listing a pattern and cancelling that listing before the persistent watch of the same pattern
    was registered lost all events of the watch on a real Nacos server. The persistent watches now
    subscribe once and read their initial keys from that subscription.
    
    A content listener is registered at most once per data ID, because the initial listing and the
    fuzzy watch events can both reach the same data ID.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Verify that the script and HTTP jobs run in native tests
    
    The script job tests only scheduled the job and shut it down, so they passed even when the
    script could not run. The HTTP job tests sent their requests to https://www.apache.org and did
    not wait for an execution either.
    
    The script job tests now wait for a successful execution in the RDB tracing events. The
    Nacos and Memory script job tests get the RDB tracing configuration for that. The HTTP job
    tests send their requests to a local HTTP server, and wait for the request and for a
    successful execution in the RDB tracing events.
    
    Add a Spring Namespace test of a job with job-type HTTP. The native test image gets the http
    URL protocol and the jdk.httpserver module.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Use a fixed port in the Spring Namespace HTTP job test
    
    The URI of the job was a placeholder, which the placeholder configurer resolves when the
    context starts. In the native image the context is built from the AOT output, where the
    properties of a namespace job are created with Map.of and are immutable, so the placeholder
    cannot be replaced, and the context fails to load with an UnsupportedOperationException.
    
    The job now sends its requests to a fixed port of the local HTTP server, and the job
    definition names that port as a literal. The test no longer needs a placeholder configurer.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Document the native coverage of HTTP jobs and the Nacos registry fix
    
    HTTP jobs defined by the Spring Namespace are covered by the native tests now. A native image
    that runs HTTP jobs needs the http URL protocol, so the documentation says so.
    
    The release notes describe the fix of the Nacos registry center.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Keep Spring Namespace job properties resolvable under native image
    
    Spring AOT generates an immutable map for the properties of a namespace job. The placeholder
    configurer replaces a placeholder in a property by changing the map in place, so a placeholder
    that is resolved at runtime failed the application context with an UnsupportedOperationException
    in the native image. A list of job listener types had the same problem.
    
    The properties and the job listener types are arrays in the bean definitions now, and
    JobConfigurationFactoryBean converts them when it builds the job configuration. Spring AOT
    generates arrays as mutable arrays, and the placeholder configurer replaces their elements.
    
    The Spring Namespace HTTP job test uses a placeholder for its URI again, so that it covers
    this case.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Keep a single release note for this pull request
    
    The native test coverage and the Nacos registry center fix are described in the pull request
    itself, so the release notes keep one entry for it.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Describe the custom job types that the native tests do not cover
    
    The built-in job types of the Spring Namespace, SCRIPT and HTTP, are covered by the native tests.
    The remaining job types are custom typed job item executors registered through SPI, which the
    native tests of ElasticJob do not cover.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Cover a custom typed job item executor registered through SPI in the native tests
    
    A job defined by the Spring Namespace with a custom job type runs the typed job item executor
    that is registered through SPI. The native tests did not cover that path.
    
    The test executor is registered in META-INF/services. Its constructors and methods are
    registered for reflection in the native image build. The job writes its RDB tracing events,
    like the other namespace jobs of the native tests.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Document the native coverage of custom job types registered through SPI
    
    The native tests cover a job with a custom job type, whose typed job item executor is
    registered through SPI. The documentation no longer lists custom job types as uncovered, and
    says that the executor class is registered for reflection in the native image build.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Describe the native test metadata of the custom executor in reachability metadata format
    
    The native test metadata moves from reflect-config.json to reachability-metadata.json, the same
    format as elasticjob-reachability-metadata. The executor is registered for reflection on the
    condition that ElasticJobExecutor is reached, because that class loads the typed job item
    executors through SPI, and the SPI service file is declared as a resource.
    
    The old file name is also the name of the reflect-config.json that Spring AOT generates into the
    same directory, so one of the two copies overwrote the other in the build output. The new name
    avoids that collision.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Remove the reachability metadata of the custom executor, which the native tests do not need
    
    The native tests pass with each of four variants of this file (no file, only the type, only the
    public constructor, and only the SPI service resource), each with 61 of 61 test cases successful
    on the GitHub runner. The variant without the file is the smallest one, so the file is removed.
    The custom executor is registered through META-INF/services and has a public no-argument
    constructor.
    
    Refs #2506
    
    * Describe the custom executor of the native tests without a reflection registration
    
    The native tests pass without reachability metadata for the custom executor, so the documentation
    no longer says that the executor class is registered for reflection in the native image build.
    
    Refs #2506

